1
00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.600
Tables are an important
element of many documents.

2
00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:05.919
In this lesson, you will
learn how to extract tables

3
00:00:05.919 --> 00:00:07.620
from documents and infer their structure.

4
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:08.929
Let's have some fun.

5
00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:13.210
Now that you've learned
how to extract information

6
00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.929
from PDFs and images using visual cues,

7
00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:19.050
in this lesson, you will learn
more advanced pre-processing

8
00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.770
In the bottom right, you can see some
financial information that's contained

9
00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:41.960
within a table that exists
within an unstructured document.

10
00:00:42.159 --> 00:00:46.450
Table extraction enables RAG
applications to extract information

11
00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:50.200
that's contained within
tables in unstructured documents.

12
00:00:51.039 --> 00:00:55.009
Some document types, such
as HTML and Word documents,

13
00:00:55.009 --> 00:00:58.539
contain table structure
information within the document itself.

14
00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.700
For example, in the table tag in HTML.

15
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:03.979
For these document types,

16
00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:07.299
you can use rules-based parsers
to extract table information.

17
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.870
For other document types,
however, such as PDFs and images,

18
00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:14.900
you need to use visual cues to
first identify the table within the

19
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:18.319
document and then process it to
extract information from the table.

20
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.750
There are a few techniques you
will learn about to accomplish this,

21
00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:24.849
table transformers, vision
transformers, and OCR pre-processing.

22
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.239
First, you'll learn
about table transformers.

23
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.060
A table transformer is a model
that identifies bounding box

24
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:45.079
for table cells and then
converts the output to HTML.

25
00:01:45.379 --> 00:01:46.799
There are two steps in this process.

26
00:01:47.439 --> 00:01:49.939
First, identify tables using
the document layout detection

27
00:01:49.939 --> 00:01:52.760
model that you learned
about in the previous lesson.

28
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.120
Then, once you've identified tables,

29
00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.040
you can route the tables
to the table transformer.

30
00:01:58.359 --> 00:02:00.420
There are some
advantages to this technique,

31
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:03.959
including the ability to trace cells
back to the original bounding boxes.

32
00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.639
You have bounding box information about
each individual cell within the table.

33
00:02:09.659 --> 00:02:13.800
The disadvantages that includes
multiple expensive model calls,

34
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:19.500
including multiple document layout
detection calls, and then multiple OCR calls.

35
00:02:19.759 --> 00:02:24.120
You can see below what the architecture
looks like for the table transformer model.

36
00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.159
And if you're interested, check
out the archive paper linked below.

37
00:02:28.599 --> 00:02:32.460
You can also extract table content
from PDFs and images using vision

38
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.000
transformers like the ones you
learned about in the previous lesson.

39
00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.419
However, unlike in the previous
lesson, when the target output was JSON,

40
00:02:41.419 --> 00:02:45.060
in this case, the
target output will be HTML.

41
00:02:45.479 --> 00:02:49.919
An advantage of this method is that it
allows for prompting, is more flexible,

42
00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:54.439
and only involves a single model call.
The disadvantage is that it's generative,

43
00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:58.240
prone to hallucination, and you don't
retain any bounding box information.

44
00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.919
For an example of what this looks like,
you can take a look at the example below.

45
00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.250
You start with the image of a document,
you run it through the Vision Transformer,

46
00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:11.599
and then on the other side, you get
the text representation of the HTML.

47
00:03:12.199 --> 00:03:15.360
A final technique that you can
use for pre-processing documents

48
00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:36.280
You'll OCR the table and then process
the table using rules-based methods.

49
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.770
An advantage is that it's
fast, and for well-behaved tables,

50
00:04:00.789 --> 00:04:03.030
And you can process this
OCR output for a table that

51
00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:04.659
you detect using a
document layout detection model.

52
00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:10.030
And you can post-process
this OCR output to construct

53
00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.460
an HTML representation of the same table.

54
00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.779
Okay, now that you've learned a
few techniques for extracting

55
00:04:17.779 --> 00:04:20.709
tables from unstructured
documents such as PDFs and images,

56
00:04:20.709 --> 00:04:23.490
you can put that into practice. First,

57
00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.629
you can import a few
of the helper functions

58
00:04:26.629 --> 00:04:29.079
that we've used in the previous lessons.

59
00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.939
Since table extraction
is model-driven, again,

60
00:04:31.939 --> 00:04:34.459
you'll use the
unstructured API because that takes

61
00:04:34.459 --> 00:04:37.620
care of all of the models set up for you.

62
00:04:38.939 --> 00:04:43.379
In the previous lesson, you saw
an example of fairly simple PDFs.

63
00:04:44.079 --> 00:04:44.980
Now you'll pre-process a more complex
document that includes tables and images.

64
00:04:50.079 --> 00:04:52.379
You can see what this
document looks like below.

65
00:04:53.819 --> 00:04:56.730
In particular, you'll be
extracting the content

66
00:04:56.730 --> 00:05:00.459
from table one within
this page of the document.

67
00:05:01.220 --> 00:05:03.379
Now that you checked out the
document, you can pre-process it.

68
00:05:04.769 --> 00:05:09.350
To pre-process it, you can pass the
document to the unstructured API. In this case,

69
00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:12.709
you should take note of a few
parameters that we're using.

70
00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.170
This parameter, infer
table structure equals true,

71
00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.990
tells the API that we want
to extract table information.

72
00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:24.189
The skip infer table types
parameter instructs the API that we

73
00:05:24.189 --> 00:05:27.500
don't want to skip table
extraction for any document types.

74
00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.160
In this case, we're
interested in the tables.

75
00:05:30.759 --> 00:05:32.920
Now you're ready to run your API call.

76
00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.680
Remember, this process requires multiple
model calls and may take a few minutes.

77
00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.370
Once the API has completed your request,

78
00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:44.129
you can filter down to just the
tables in the document using a similar

79
00:05:44.129 --> 00:05:48.879
filter operation to what
you've seen earlier in the course.

80
00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:53.240
In this case, we're going to look for
elements that have the category table.

81
00:05:55.339 --> 00:05:57.189
Now that you have your table element,

82
00:05:57.189 --> 00:06:00.089
you can see the text of
the table in the document

83
00:06:00.089 --> 00:06:01.939
using the text attribute on the element.

84
00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:05.949
As you learned earlier, however,

85
00:06:05.949 --> 00:06:09.910
it's also helpful to have an HTML
representation of the table so that you

86
00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:13.310
can pass the information to an LLM
while maintaining the table structure.

87
00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:18.620
This information is
available in the text as HTML

88
00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:22.310
field within the
element metadata for tables.

89
00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:27.579
You can use this code block to view what
the HTML in the metadata field looks like.

90
00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:33.379
As you can see, this is an HTML
representation of the table in the document.

91
00:06:38.939 --> 00:06:41.740
If you're interested in
displaying this table,

92
00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:46.540
you can also do that using the
HTML display function within IPython.

93
00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.269
Once you've extracted
the table content from the

94
00:06:57.269 --> 00:06:59.920
document and converted
the table content to HTML,

95
00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:04.889
it can be helpful to summarize these
tables so that you can search on these

96
00:07:04.889 --> 00:07:08.199
tables when you perform similarity
search within a RAG architecture.

97
00:07:08.459 --> 00:07:12.149
To do this, we will use a
few utilities from LangChain,

98
00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:17.310
You can also learn more
about LangChain through

99
00:07:29.699 --> 00:07:32.439
Once you have imported
these helper functions,

100
00:07:32.439 --> 00:07:34.800
you can instantiate
the summarization chain

101
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.540
and then summarize the table HTML content.

102
00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:44.100
As you can see, the model
successfully summarized the table.

103
00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:49.420
There is an HTML representation of the
table as well as a summary that you can

104
00:07:49.420 --> 00:07:53.240
use to search over when you perform
similarity search within your RAG system.

105
00:07:53.819 --> 00:07:57.139
Now that you know how to extract
table content from PDFs and images,

106
00:07:57.139 --> 00:07:59.439
try it on a few of your own files.

107
00:08:00.199 --> 00:08:03.459
If you're interested in playing
around with a few parameters,

108
00:08:03.459 --> 00:08:07.699
also try changing the Hi-Res Model
Name parameter to Chipper so that you

109
00:08:07.699 --> 00:08:10.639
can compare vision transformer
outputs to table transformer outputs.

110
00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:14.639
In the next lesson, we'll put all
of your skills together to build

111
00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:14.879
a Rag bot of your own that
works on a variety of data types.